There are two SHOULD items where I see a real branch in the road and I shouldn't pick for you. Quick answers and I'll write the rest.

### 1. CORS / proxy strategy

You have `next dev` on `:3000` calling the API on `:5080`. In the browser this is cross-origin; CORS will bite you the moment the API has any cookie-based auth (wave 3) and will at minimum spam OPTIONS preflights in dev.

Two options:

| Option | Tradeoff |
|---|---|
| **A. Next.js `rewrites()` proxy** — `next.config.ts` maps `/api/*` → `http://localhost:5080/api/*`; client always uses relative `/api` URLs. | One source of truth. Devs hit same-origin. CORS disappears. Standard. |
| **B. CORS on the backend** — add `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Cors` to the API in wave 2, allow `http://localhost:3000`. | CORS config lives in the API. Devs and prod see the same wire. More moving parts. |

My recommendation: **A** (rewrites). CORS only matters when a different origin needs the resource, and a Next dev server proxy is one line in config.

